# THE PIN IS A DIGEST OVER WORKING-TREE BYTES, so any checkout that rewrites a
# byte breaks it. `core.autocrlf=true` is the default on Git for Windows, and it
# rewrites LF to CRLF for every file Git guesses is text — which changed the
# digest of all 172 tracked files at once and made `pack_pin.py` report
# PACK PIN NOT VERIFIED against a checkout of the correct revision.
#
# Reproduced before this file existed:
#
#     git -c core.autocrlf=true clone --branch <b> <repo> c && cd c
#     python3 tools/pack_pin.py .
#     -> tree digest 17c73eebe349749e… does not match the manifest's 6404bbe8…
#
# `-text` tells Git that no file here is subject to end-of-line conversion, so a
# checkout is byte-identical to the stored blob under every `core.autocrlf` and
# `core.eol` setting, on every platform.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, because an in-tree attributes file is not the last word:
# `$GIT_DIR/info/attributes` outranks this file in gitattributes precedence, and a
# smudge filter attached there will still rewrite bytes at checkout. Demonstrated
# in two commands by an independent reader, against this very repository. That is
# a local override a consumer chooses, not something this repository can declare
# away — and `pack_pin.py` reports it as a digest mismatch, which is the right
# answer, if an unhelpful one. Fixing it here rather than by normalising inside the digest
# is deliberate: a digest that silently ignores some bytes is not a digest of the
# tree, and deciding WHICH bytes to ignore needs a text/binary vocabulary — the
# kind of open-ended list this release removed elsewhere.
#
# Held by tools/tests/test_release_integrity.py, which asks Git itself whether
# any tracked path still has the `text` attribute set.
* -text
